def build_identity():
	"""Short hash of this file, so two copies can be told apart.

    VERSION answers "which release is this", and deliberately: it is stated
    three times -- here, in the VERSION file and in debian/changelog -- and
    `make version-check' gates that they agree. It cannot also answer "is
    the fmake I am running the same one as over there", because that would
    mean changing it on every edit, and a package version that moves eighty
    times in a fortnight is not a version.

    They are different questions and the second one had no answer at all.
    An installed copy eighty-six commits behind the source reported the
    same `fmake 1.0' as the source did, so a caller had no way to tell
    which it had invoked -- and the difference was twenty-three silently
    dropped targets. That was reported from a project that hit it, and its
    words for it are the right ones: not that the failure was silent, but
    that there was no check to make.

    Hashing the file itself rather than stamping something at build time,
    because there is no build step to stamp: `make all' produces the man
    page and the completion, and fmake *is* the source. Adding a build step
    to carry an identity would trade away the property that makes this one
    file copyable anywhere, which is worth more than the identity. Any copy
    can answer for itself, however it arrived -- package, `cp', symlink or
    worktree -- and two copies that differ differ visibly.

    Never fatal. A version string is the last place to raise, so an
    unreadable file gives `unknown' rather than a traceback from -V.
    """
	try:
		with open(os.path.realpath(__file__), "rb") as fh:
			return hashlib.sha256(fh.read()).hexdigest()[:8]
	except OSError:
		return "unknown"

